(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-4-hypotension-quiz"]={"title":"Hypotension Quiz 1 — CMS I Exam 4","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 4","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which features characterize acute hypotension?","choices":["Waxing, waning, intermittent symptoms","Gradual onset with no symptoms ever","Persistent lows from an unavoidable cause","Sudden onset, symptoms, a specific trigger"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Acute hypotension is of sudden onset, usually symptomatic and usually with specific triggers, unlike chronic hypotension, which waxes and wanes. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which cause of hypotension is typically acute?","choices":["Cardiomyopathy","Heart failure","Ventricular pump weakness","Toxic exposure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Toxic exposure and medication-mediated overdose produce acute hypotension of sudden onset with a specific trigger. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"How is postural orthostatic tachycardia syndrome defined in adults?","choices":["Heart rate up over 10 when lying down","Heart rate up over 30, or above 120, in 10 minutes","Systolic fall over 20 mmHg in 3 minutes","Heart rate up over 30, or above 120, in 3 minutes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The heart rate rises by more than 30 beats per minute, or exceeds 120, within 10 minutes of standing, and usually improves when lying down. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"In whom is vasovagal syncope most commonly seen?","choices":["Adults with diabetes","Young women","Older men","Adolescent boys"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Vasovagal syncope, the common faint, is most commonly seen in young women, and its triggers include fear, pain, heat and prolonged standing. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which type of reflex syncope is known as the common faint?","choices":["Situational syncope","Neurogenic syncope","Cardiogenic syncope","Vasovagal syncope"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Vasovagal syncope is the most common type, known as the common faint, and is most commonly seen in young women. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What should a patient with recurrent reflex syncope be asked to keep?","choices":["A weekly weight chart","A home blood pressure average","A log of pattern and frequency","A daily blood glucose diary"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A patient log helps determine the pattern and frequency of episodes, which supports a diagnosis built mainly on the history. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Why should a single low reading be interpreted cautiously?","choices":["Pressure never varies within a day","Each reading is a snapshot in time","Cuffs overestimate low pressures","Low readings are always artifact"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A reading is a snapshot in time, and it must also be weighed against age, comorbidities and the possibility of relative hypotension. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which history favors reflex syncope over a seizure?","choices":["Brief prodrome, then rapid recovery","Tongue biting and long confusion","Rhythmic jerking and incontinence","No warning, then palpitations"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A brief prodrome of warmth, nausea, sweating and lightheadedness followed by rapid recovery favors reflex syncope over seizure or a primary arrhythmia. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which cause of hypotension is classed as chronic?","choices":["Bacteremia","Cardiomyopathy","Medication overdose","Toxic exposure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Cardiogenic causes, reduced ejection fraction heart failure and cardiomyopathy, produce chronic hypotension, whose cause may be unavoidable. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"How should a suspected vascular cause of syncope be evaluated?","choices":["Electively, with an outpatient monitor","Routinely, with a symptom log","Later, with a tilt-table test","Emergently, in the emergency department"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Vascular causes, dissection, aneurysm, stroke and pulmonary embolism, need emergent testing in the emergency department plus cardiology consultation. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Why is hypotension not the final diagnosis in a patient who faints?","choices":["It is defined only by the symptoms","It results from an underlying cause","It is too rare a finding to name","It always resolves before assessment"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Syncope is the result of symptomatic hypotension, and treatment is aimed at the underlying cause, the real diagnosis, such as volume loss, a medication, a reflex or a cardiac problem. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"In a patient with a normal baseline pressure, which systolic change on standing meets the orthostatic criterion?","choices":["A fall of more than 20 mmHg within 10 minutes","A heart rate rise of more than 30 per minute","A fall of more than 20 mmHg within 3 minutes","A fall of more than 10 mmHg within 3 minutes"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A systolic fall of more than 20 mmHg within 3 minutes of standing meets the criterion; a diastolic fall of more than 10 mmHg within 3 minutes is the companion criterion. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which of these is a symptom rather than a sign?","choices":["A heart rate of 148","Dry oral mucous membranes","Lightheadedness","A standing pressure drop"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Lightheadedness is a subjective change that the patient feels and reports, whereas signs are objective and measurable, such as vital signs. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Who is typically affected by postural orthostatic tachycardia syndrome?","choices":["Infants, equally both sexes","Over 60, with Parkinson disease","Ages 15 to 50, more often women","Over 70, more often men"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The syndrome affects people aged 15 to 50, usually women more than men, and is associated with Ehlers-Danlos syndrome. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which of these is a cause of non-neurogenic orthostatic hypotension?","choices":["Advanced age","Ehlers-Danlos syndrome","Emotional triggers","Autoimmune disorders"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Advanced age is listed with hypovolemia and medication as a cause of non-neurogenic orthostatic hypotension in the elderly. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which systolic threshold applies when baseline pressure is elevated with supine hypertension (high pressure while lying flat)?","choices":["A fall of more than 30 mmHg","A fall of more than 20 mmHg","A fall of more than 10 mmHg","A fall of more than 40 mmHg"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. With an elevated baseline or supine hypertension the systolic fall must exceed 30 mmHg within 3 minutes of standing to meet the orthostatic criterion. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which subtypes are recognized within reflex syncope?","choices":["Vasodepressor, cardioinhibitory and mixed","Neurogenic, non-neurogenic and mixed","Arrhythmic, structural and vascular","Primary, secondary and drug-induced"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The standard syncope classification splits reflex syncope by whether the vessels dilate, the heart slows, or both, all under an inappropriate reflex. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What separates near-syncope from syncope?","choices":["Recovery that needs medical treatment","Warning symptoms without losing consciousness","A longer loss of consciousness than syncope","A cardiac rather than a reflex cause"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Near-syncope, or presyncope, is the dizziness, lightheadedness and tunnel vision that precede a faint; syncope adds the transient loss of consciousness and postural tone. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which feature is often present in neurogenic but usually absent in non-neurogenic orthostatic hypotension?","choices":["Volume depletion","A marked heart rate rise","Recent diuretic use","Autonomic symptoms"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Autonomic symptoms are often present with autonomic failure and usually absent when the cause is volume loss or medication. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which test evaluates structural cardiogenic syncope?","choices":["Echocardiogram","Tilt-table test","Symptom log","Orthostatic vitals"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Structural causes, infarction, cardiomyopathy, heart failure and valve disease, are evaluated with echocardiography or stress echocardiography plus cardiology consultation. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Where are vasopressors such as norepinephrine and vasopressin used for hypotension?","choices":["Outpatient use for the tachycardia syndrome","Emergency department and intensive care only","Primary care for chronic hypotension","Home use for recurrent reflex syncope"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Epinephrine, norepinephrine, dopamine, phenylephrine and vasopressin are listed for the emergency department and intensive care unit only. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which drug is best used with neurogenic syncope?","choices":["Midodrine","Fludrocortisone","Droxidopa","Vasopressin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Midodrine is best with neurogenic syncope, but it is avoided with supine hypertension, severe heart disease and chronic kidney disease. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What defines non-neurogenic orthostatic syncope?","choices":["A reflex faint after a bodily function","A heart rate rise without a pressure drop","A postural drop without autonomic failure","A postural drop from autonomic nerve damage"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It is a sustained fall in pressure on standing or sitting upright that is not a result of autonomic dysfunction, usually from hypovolemia, medication or advanced age. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which three groups make up the most common causes of syncope?","choices":["Neurogenic, septic and toxic","Situational, vasovagal and seizure","Arrhythmic, structural and anxiety","Orthostatic, reflex and cardiogenic"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Orthostatic syncope divides into neurogenic and non-neurogenic, reflex syncope into situational and vasovagal, and cardiogenic syncope into arrhythmia, structural and vascular. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Why is fludrocortisone avoided in heart failure?","choices":["It worsens kidney function","It causes salt and water retention","It causes supine hypertension","It slows the heart rate sharply"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Fludrocortisone causes salt and water retention, which helps the tachycardia syndrome but is why it is avoided in congestive heart failure. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which measures are first-line for orthostatic syncope?","choices":["Vasopressors started in primary care","Fludrocortisone for every patient","Strict fluid restriction every day","Slow position changes, treat the cause"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Slow positional changes limit the drop on standing, and treating the underlying cause addresses the real diagnosis behind the hypotension. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which conditions are examples of neurogenic orthostatic hypotension causes?","choices":["Emotional stress and pain","Arrhythmia and valve disease","Diabetes and Parkinson disease","Diarrhea and hemorrhage"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Both, like multiple system atrophy, damage the autonomic nervous system, which is why these patients show a pressure fall with only a minimal heart rate rise on standing. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Inadequate venous return causing orthostatic hypotension has which two sources?","choices":["Volume depletion and venous pooling","Primary and secondary autonomic failure","Arrhythmia and structural heart disease","Emotional stress and prolonged standing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In the standard classification these lower cardiac output on standing, while structural or drug-induced autonomic failure lowers peripheral resistance instead. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which quick test excludes hypoglycemia as the cause of a faint?","choices":["A blood glucose level","A tilt-table test","An echocardiogram","Orthostatic vital signs"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A normal blood glucose rules out hypoglycemia, one of the non-hypotensive causes of syncope, alongside blood counts and a metabolic panel for infection or metabolic causes. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"When hypotension is caused by a medication, what is the first step?","choices":["Start fludrocortisone therapy","Refer for a tilt-table test","Reduce or remove the medication","Add midodrine to counter it"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The principle is to remove the offending agent or trigger: iatrogenic hypotension is treated by reducing or removing the responsible medication. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 23"}]};
